# zenjxl-decoder NEON vs no-SIMD — first ARM measurement
date:    2026-07-28
host:    Apple M4 Pro (12 core), macOS, aarch64
build:   release, NO -C target-cpu=native
method:  COMPILE-TIME A/B on the feature set (SIMD here is feature-gated, not
         runtime-token-gated: `neon = ["jxl_simd/neon"]`, inside the default
         `all-simd`):
           cargo bench -p zenjxl-decoder --bench decode_bench                     # NEON
           cargo bench -p zenjxl-decoder --bench decode_bench --no-default-features # scalar

## Result — the split is by coding mode, not by size

| image                      | mode    | NEON     | no-SIMD  | ratio |
|----------------------------|---------|----------|----------|-------|
| bicycles_web_q85           | VarDCT  |  13.8ms  |  25.2ms  | 1.83x |
| green_queen_vardct_e3      | VarDCT  |   7.3ms  |  12.5ms  | 1.71x |
| issue648_palette0          | modular | 106.4ms  | 112.0ms  | 1.05x |
| green_queen_modular_e3     | modular |  17.7ms  |  18.0ms  | 1.02x |
| grayscale_patches_modular  | modular |   9.8ms  |   8.4ms  | 0.86x |

VarDCT (lossy, DCT + chroma-from-luma + upsampling) gains 1.7-1.8x. Modular
(lossless, predictor + entropy coded) gains essentially nothing — consistent
with the pattern seen elsewhere in this sweep: entropy/predictor-serial codecs
resist vectorisation (heic ~1.00x, CABAC-bound; zenjpeg 1.3x, Huffman-bound).

## The one negative: grayscale_patches_modular, 0.86x

NEON 9.8ms +/-0.7 vs no-SIMD 8.4ms +/-0.4 — the error bars are close but do not
overlap, so this is probably real rather than noise, and it is modest. Enabling
SIMD makes this case ~14% slower.

NOT DIAGNOSED. Plausibly the same shape as the ultrahdr finding recorded the
same day (a LUT/gather-bound kernel where NEON's lack of a gather instruction
makes the vector path lose), but that is a hypothesis — it has not been
profiled, and one image is not enough to characterise the modular path. Anyone
picking this up should first widen the modular corpus to see whether 0.86x is
this image or the mode.

Since default features include NEON, this case ships slower on ARM today. It is
one image out of five and the VarDCT wins are much larger, so this is recorded
as a lead rather than a reason to change defaults.


## RESOLVED (same day) — the grayscale_patches_modular "14% regression" was not real

The open item above reported `grayscale_patches_modular` at 0.86x (9.8ms NEON
vs 8.4ms no-SIMD) and flagged it as probably real, with "widen the modular
corpus" as the next step. That was done, and it dissolves the finding.

The modular group went from 2 images to 8 (every modular fixture in
resources/test). Re-measured, same method, same host:

| image                              | NEON     | no-SIMD  | ratio |
|------------------------------------|----------|----------|-------|
| green_queen_modular_e3             |  16.70ms |  16.70ms | 1.00x |
| issue648_palette0                  | 105.20ms | 111.10ms | 1.06x |
| grayscale_patches_modular          |   7.60ms |   7.60ms | 1.00x |
| small_grayscale_patches_modular    |   0.80ms |   0.80ms | 1.00x |
| gray_alpha_lossless                |   0.20ms |   0.20ms | 1.00x |

grayscale_patches_modular is 1.00x — no regression. Note its ABSOLUTE time also
moved, 9.8ms -> 7.6ms, purely from being benched in the `modular` group instead
of the `small` group. Nothing about the decoder changed between those two runs.

So the 0.86x was benchmark-group context, not a NEON defect. This is the second
false regression of the day traced to cross-group comparison (the other:
linear-srgb's unpremultiply ~6%). Both were found by re-testing rather than by
inspecting code, and neither would have been caught by trusting the first
number.

Standing conclusion for this crate, now on 8 modular images rather than 2:
VarDCT gains 1.71-1.83x from NEON, modular gains 1.00-1.06x, and there is NO
case where SIMD is slower. The modular result is expected — that path is
predictor + entropy coded and serial by nature.

METHOD NOTE: do not compare a single image across two zenbench groups. Group
composition shifts absolute timings materially (30% here). Comparisons must be
made WITHIN one group, which is what the widened `modular` group now permits.

## Follow-up 2026-07-31: WHY modular gains nothing (and why that is correct)

The split above is real and the modular side is not a missing-SIMD gap — it is
a dependency-bound path. Audited rather than assumed:

  frame/modular/predict.rs         644 lines   0 SIMD references
  frame/modular/mod.rs            1302 lines   0
  frame/modular/decode/channel.rs  217 lines   0
  entropy_coding/ans.rs            504 lines   0
  entropy_coding/huffman.rs        626 lines   0
  entropy_coding/decode.rs         829 lines   0

Two structural blockers, both inherent to the format rather than to this port:

1. **ANS / Huffman entropy decode is serial by construction** — each symbol's
   state depends on the previous one. No lane parallelism exists at this level.
2. **The modular predictor chains horizontally.** `PredictionContext` carries
   `left`, `leftleft`, `topleft`, `topright` — each pixel's prediction depends
   on the pixel decoded immediately to its left, so the x-loop cannot be
   vectorized without changing the format's semantics.

So 1.02-1.05x is close to the ceiling for this coding mode, in the same sense
that zenraw's normalize_uniform (44 GB/s) and garb's copy kernels (55 GB/s) are
at *their* ceilings — the limit is structural, not an un-written kernel.

Where headroom would actually come from, if this is ever revisited: coarse-grain
parallelism (per-group / per-channel), not per-pixel SIMD. VarDCT already gets
1.71-1.83x precisely because its inverse transform has no such chain.

Do not read "0 SIMD references in the modular path" as work waiting to be done.
